WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:06.950 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  [Music]  the tale that i'm about to tell you  might be the strangest thing you're  going to hear today in the rocky heart  of central asia nearly a century ago an  english pickle manufacturer briefly  became the king of a breakaway nation  he was born bertram shell drake but was  later known as the pickle king of  tartary  this is the story of how it all happened  [Music]  bertram sheldrake was born in london in  1888 the same year that rudyard kipling  published the man who would be king a  fictional story of how two british  adventurers became the kings of  kefiristan a remote region in northern  afghanistan their good fortune turns  however as the pair are slowly corrupted  by their newfound power following a riot  they are violently overthrown by their  afghan subjects as we shall see  sometimes reality is stranger than  fiction  bertram's father gosling had made his  living as a manufacturer and distributor  of pickles  you know cucumbers left to ferment in  vinegar due to the british public's lack  of taste buds it was a successful  operation  soon g-shell drake's pickles branched  out into ketchup chutney sauces capers  vinegar and curries basically anything  to go with fish and chips on the back of  his father's pickle money bertram was  able to attend a private boarding school  it was during this time that he was  introduced to the philosophy and  teachings of islam and its principles of  peaceful service to god and humankind  this chance introduction would become  pivotal for young bertram for at a young  age of 15 he renounced his catholic  faith and converted renaming himself  khalid a central practice in islam is  helping those who are less fortunate a  principal known as zakat having now  inherited his father's pickle business  khalid cheltrick donated much of his  earnings to charity he also helped fund  one of britain's first purpose-built  mosques which opened in monsworth south  london  sheldrick's philanthropy had earned him  a reputation but british newspapers  could not understand why this well-to-do  gentleman had adopted islam  tabloid articles and magazines were  known to mock  in response sheldrake wrote a letter to  king george v about the abuse that many  british muslims undergo in this letter  he called for the end of the many quote  cartoons in which the muslim faith and  its prophet were made fun of but  sheldrake's reputation would go truly  international when he assisted in the  conversion of gladys palmer the daughter  of a wealthy biscuit magnate by the way  what's with this story and snacks  he performed the ceremony aboard a  chartered airliner flight above the  english channel reciting the shahada  over the din of the plane's engines  newspapers went into a frenzy over this  and not just in the uk the story was  covered in magazines across europe  america and the middle east  news reached the furthest of places  including an often forgotten region in  central asia a place known to many  english speakers at the time as tartary  roughly 3600 miles from london in the  far western corner of china is an arid  and rocky province known as xinjiang  because of its harsh climate very few  people live here but those that do are  known as the uyghurs a turkic people  followers of islam with their own unique  language and culture at the center of  this region is the historic capital  kashgar for centuries it was the  northern hub of the silk roads a vast  intersecting network of trade routes  that ran from beijing to byzantium as  travellers passed through the city camel  caravan in tow they exchanged exotic  goods from foreign lands as a sheer  coincidence to our story one of these  goods included pickles with cucumbers  traded from india early chinese builders  were some of the first to preserve the  mint vinegar during the construction of  the great wall these pickled cucumbers  could be placed in jars and so were  readily portable the perfect snack for a  silk road trip it was via this ancient  trade network that pickles first arrived  in france somewhere between the 8th and  9th centuries ever since they've become  staples in several european cuisines  anyway because of kashgar's lucrative  position along the silk road this region  has passed through the hands of its  larger neighbours in the 13th century it  was a vassal state to the mongol hordes  under chingas khan and by the 18th  century it was ruled by the chinese qing  dynasty who made it its westernmost  province fast forward to 1933 and there  was an immense period of upheaval in  china a civil war between the  nationalists and the communists it was  amid this fragmenting state that the  people of xinjiang saw an opportunity to  break away  with the city of kashgar as its capital  uyga nationals swiftly declared the  turkic islamic republic of east  turkestan but recorded the etr for short  the fledgling state wanted recognition  on the world stage and one way to do  this is with a popular figurehead the  story goes that various uyghur groups  could not decide on a leader amongst  their own people and so they agreed that  the leader had to be from the outside it  just so happened that at this time news  about a prominent convert from england  had reached kashgar they had found a  suitable leader and a delegation was  sent to london  as a quick side note it's unknown  whether any of this was actually known  to the etr's president a man called kojo  niaz most of what remains about this  story are from western newspapers from  the time and so they may contain some  fanciful elements either way we do know  that when the delegation arrived to  south london home calachel drake eagerly  accepted their invitation he renounced  his former life as a pickle manufacturer  and accepted the title of his majesty  king khalid of islamistan shortly  thereafter sheldrake joined his future  subjects as they boarded a ferry to hong  kong hearing news of this remarkable  story british tabloids came up with all  sorts of names from the english mere of  kashgar to lord of the rooftop of the  world but there's one that really stuck  the pickle king of tartary  having arrived in beijing by the spring  of 1934 the soon to be king khalid  prepared to make the roughly 2 000 mile  land journey to kashgar along the  ancient paths that once made up the silk  road however the moment the chinese  nationalist government heard of their  rogue province's plans to install a  pickle king they were suspicious what  was a british man who had never been to  xinjiang doing accepting the title of  king the soviets to the northern border  accused sheldrake of being an agent of  the crown making xinjiang just another  colony of the british empire i mean are  we to blame them for thinking this at  the time britain was still intoxicated  with its ill-gotten empire with an  estimated quarter of the world's  population under its dominion  even the etr's pals weren't too pleased  about the prospect of a pickle king the  neighbouring kingdom of afghanistan who  had once helped this breakaway republic  break away withdrew their political  support as he was on route to kashgar  khalid sheldrake was totally unaware of  this he had no communication with the  outside world when he finally arrived in  august it was clear that the etr was  already in a right royal pickle and not  the one they'd been expecting a joint  effort between the chinese nationalists  and the soviets had quashed the uyghur  rebellion and reclaimed xinjiang with  the dream of his kingdom collapsing  before his eyes and fearing his own  safety sheldrake fled and made his way  to british occupied india  soon khalid sheltrak returned to england  where he lived as a king in exile he  resumed his philanthropy work and  continued to raise funds for muslim  charities and build new mosques in his  spare time he was known to give lectures  on the current state of xinjiang as well  as the uyghur people for many however it  was just simply too far away to be of  interest they were more preoccupied with  the news surrounding an austrian art  school reject and his national socialist  party in germany  after the second world war ended  sheldrick lived out his days in his  south london home dying in 1947 at the  relatively young age of 59.  after his death this bizarre story faded  into obscurity like a cucumber in brine  this apocryphal tale from central asia  is preserved in only a few surviving  articles sheldrick's moniker the pickle  king of tartary has an amusing ring to  it and when i first heard the story i  thought i'd be making a light-hearted  video but i think this story is more  complicated than that and i suspect  depending on your own life experiences  you'll react to it in a number of ways  you might see khalid sheldrake as the  charitable philanthropist a patron of  britain's muslim communities and someone  who called out bigotry to the highest  authorities in the land but you might  also see it as a story of empires and  colonies and a naive man at the center  of it like rudyard kipling's cautionary  tale who himself was a staunch  imperialist by the way you may ask  whether it's ever right to rule the  place if you've never even been since  the chinese civil war ended in 1949  xinjiang has been a province in the  people's republic of china however in  recent years there have been some  allegations coming out of xinjiang about  the various human rights abuses against  the uyghur people although something  that the chinese communist party has  vehemently denied as it stands the  situation is very much ongoing and if  you want to learn more about this i  provided links in the description below  the pickle king of tartary is a strange  footnote in the often turbulent history  of central asia to my mind the story  about how a pickle manufacturer nearly  became a king is certainly one of those  obscure topics that deserve more  attention  [Music]  hey thanks for watching if you're new  here and want to see more why not  subscribe a like and a comment also go a  really long way anyway i look forward to  seeing you next time  goodbye

